    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nHonda Details 2028 CR-V's 2.0L Hybrid Powertrain With Available DualPower eAWD\n13 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nHonda's next-generation hybrid includes a 2.0-liter naturally aspirated inline-four mill that promises to be more efficient than the CR-V Hybrid's outgoing engine. To be deployed in a multitude of models, including the 2028 model year CR-V, the redesigned hybrid system includes an engine that burns fuel more efficiently, maintaining stoichiometric combustion across a much wider operating range.\nOfficially branded Honda Hybrid, which is as clinical as it gets in this industry, the new setup further includes a familiar two-motor drive unit, improved thermal management, and an optional electric rear axle. Honda detailed this hybrid system after introducing the second-generation Avancier , namely a sport utility vehicle with three-row seating that Honda assembles in Thailand rather than Japan.\nAs far as the combustion engine is concerned, stoichiometric combustion is the name of the game. The ideal air-to-fuel ratio sees all oxygen and gasoline reacting completely without excess oxygen or unburned fuel. That's just around 14.7:1 by mass for gasoline, a ratio that allows the cats to clean exhaust gases effectively.\nCurrent-gen engines allow extra fuel under high output to either cool parts or boost power. Honda prefers to speed up combustion rather than relying on richer mixtures to manage either problem. How did the Japanese automaker do it? For starters, the intake port was redesigned, narrowing the relatively straight shape while adding a ski jump-like ramp at its base.\nThis increases airflow velocity while generating a stronger vertical tumble flow inside the cylinders. The rolling motion of the incoming air-fuel charge is preserved as the piston approaches top dead center, thus creating the turbulence needed to accelerate flame propagation after the spark ignites the mixture. The piston crown has also been revised to retain more of that tumble movement.\nPhoto: Honda Honda also developed a two-piece water jacket designed to cool both sides of the exhaust port, helping control thermal loading around the combustion chamber. This water jacket further complements the faster burn achieved through the redesigned intake and piston crowns.\nOptimized for hybrid applications rather than gasoline-only and hybrid applications, the new engine features a lighter block. The aluminum-alloy block weighs approximately 900 grams (2 pounds) less, while the crankshaft is 500 grams (1 pound) lighter. Revised pin-boss areas, piston skirts, and other components contribute further reductions.\nSimilar to the outgoing hybrids, the front drive unit retains the parallel-axis arrangement for its generator and traction motors. The new unit is lighter and reduces friction through improved lubrication, bearings, and oil temperature management. Even the magnetic circuits in both motors have been revised, with the generator priding itself on more torque density while using fewer magnets.\nIts traction motor uses magnets devoid of heavy rare-earth elements, while thinner electromagnetic steel sheets reduce electrical losses. The power control unit is shorter front to rear by 51 millimeters (2 inches) and 3 percent lighter to boot. Said control unit integrates additional control hardware for the electric AWD system, including the rear drive motor's control chip.\nDualPower eAWD implies a 50-kW rear drive unit, with 50 kilowatts converting to either 68 metric horsepower or 67 mechanical ones. During high-speed cruising, an engine direct-drive gear allows the combustion engine to drive the front wheels mechanically.\nA water-cooled oil cooler transfers heat between the engine's coolant circuit and the drive unit's lubricating oil. The revised system integrates the cooling circuits for both the front drive unit and the power control unit, which are separate on the 2027 model year CR-V Hybrid.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda Acura Hybrid SUV US\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT MCACN 2026: Chrysler 300 Display Brings Together All Letter Series Models (1955-1965)",
